        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "3. A combination of environmental and political factors caused the rapid decline of the Mayan civilization in the ninth century, most likely. Severe economic distress resulted from rapid population growth and prolonged drought in this period. In addition, the Maya's ability to respond to these circumstances was hindered by constant warfare and a rigid system of monarchic rule. Much of the Maya's territory was almost entirely depopulated within the century.",
